<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nahal Hever</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cave of Letters</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nahal Hever is a dry ravine in the Judaean Desert running down to the western shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>. In its Cave of Letters, excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yigael-yadin">Yigael Yadin</a> in 1960–1961, refugees of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt left letters from Simon bar Kokhba, legal documents and the private archive of a woman named Babatha, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, Nabataean and Greek.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahal-hever.jpg" alt="Nahal Hever" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tsachior · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D7%AA%D7%A6%D7%A4%D7%99%D7%AA_%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A0%D7%97%D7%9C_%D7%97%D7%91%D7%A8.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
